An edition of a classic or sacred text where the original is
accompanied by layers of comments is one of the most ancient types of
books.  One of the most prominent examples is the traditional layout of the
Talmud, where the original text is surrounded by the comments.
Usually this commented edition is
completely different from another subgenre of academic books: a
facsimile edition, which faithfully reproduces the original.  In the
latter case the pages are typeset exactly as in the historical book,
and there are no footnote markers in the text, because it would break
the integrity of the page.  Instead, sometimes endnotes are added,
which refer to the page numbers in the main corpus.

